#* {{quote-journal|en|author=“F.”|title=Orkney Charms|journal={{w|Notes and Queries}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:George Bell (publisher)|George Bell]]|date=16 September 1854|volume=X|issue=225|page=221|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NbFCAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA221|oclc=42717382|passage=When a healthy child suddenly becomes sickly, and no one can account for the change, the child is said to be "forespoken." Or when a stout man or woman becomes hypochondriac, or affected with nervous complaints, he or she is "forespoken." '''Some one''' has perhaps said "He's a bonny bairn," or "Thou ar' lookin weel the day;" but they have spoken with an ''ill tongue''. They have neglected to add, "God save the bairn," or, "Safe be thou," &c.}}
